# Performance & Reliability

Follow these on every change. Prefer existing patterns (`ListPageItems.ReplaceAll`, `PagedListViewModel`/`NumberedListViewModel`,
`ClinicContext` single-flight, `ILoadablePage.Deactivate`, `PerformanceMonitor`, `SearchBox` Enter-to-search).

## UI thread and render
- No sync I/O, `.Result` / `.Wait()` / `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`, or heavy CPU in constructors, property getters,
  converters, `OnRender`, `MeasureOverride`/`ArrangeOverride`, or layout callbacks. Offload with `async` and marshal
  back via `Dispatcher.UIThread.Post`/`InvokeAsync`.
- Property getters and converters stay O(1) and allocation-light; no LINQ, formatting, or I/O there.
- Do not touch Avalonia controls or `ObservableCollection` from background threads; post to the UI thread.
- Timers, polling, and background loops run only while needed: stop or pause on `Deactivate` / detach / sign-out
  (same idea as `ConnectionMonitor` while signed in and page `Deactivate` cancelling in-flight reads).

## Lists, bindings, XAML
- Lists: use `DataTable`/`DataGrid` (virtualizing). Never put an items control inside `ScrollViewer` or `StackPanel` in a
  way that disables virtualization; do not use a non-virtualizing panel for long lists; keep item templates light.
- Compiled bindings stay on: every view/`DataTemplate` has `x:DataType`; fix broken paths; expensive work does not belong
  in converters. `{l:Money}`, `{l:TrKey}` and `{l:Tr ..., Arg=}` take a nested `{Binding}` so they stay compiled too.
- Forms: only properties named like request fields (or listed in `AffectsRequest`) rebuild the request for `IsDirty`.
- Prefer `{StaticResource}` unless the value must track theme (`Brush*`, theme shadows → `{DynamicResource}`). Do not
  duplicate resource dictionaries or grow broad style selectors without need.
- Keep XAML nesting shallow; avoid expensive effects (blur, heavy shadows, elaborate geometries) unless a token/pattern
  already uses them.
- Small lists that are already loaded (e.g. the New Order catalog) are filtered in memory with `SearchText.Normalize`.
- Search/filter that hits the API or rebuilds a large list: debounce/throttle, or Enter-to-search (`ui:SearchBox`).
  Do not fire a network/reload on every keystroke.

## Collections and change notification
- Do not add/remove/`PropertyChanged` per item in a tight loop. Batch: `ListPageItems.ReplaceAll` / `PrependNewest`, or
  build then assign once. High-frequency updates need throttling.
- Menu pages: reuse view models for the portal visit (`PageHost` caches the view); do not recreate on every navigation.
  Services are singletons; page VMs are Transient from DI but held by the shell. Sub-pages stay transient unless an
  existing reuse pattern (New order / Order details) applies. Do not invent a second lifetime scheme.

## Data loading and caching
- Load on first need (or page `ActivateAsync`), not at app/portal startup. Prefer paging over full dumps; only fetch
  columns/fields the UI shows. Prefetch only where an existing pattern does (`ClinicContext` on Admin portal).
- Cache repeated identical reads with clear ownership and invalidation (see `ClinicContext`, `LabTestCatalog` + catalog version). No
  unbounded caches; clear on sign-out / edit / version bump.
- Do not keep large objects (images, full result sets, bitmaps) longer than needed; dispose `IDisposable`s.

## Allocations and hot paths
- Avoid LINQ-in-loops, repeated string concatenation, boxing, and unnecessary `ToList()`/`ToArray()` on hot paths
  (render, layout, per-keystroke, per-row bind, tight server loops).
- Logging: no string interpolation/formatting for disabled levels on hot paths; do not log per-frame or per-row.
  Use `PerformanceMonitor` for speed debugging — do not sprinkle stopwatches in view models.

## Async, exceptions, startup
- `async Task` (or `async ValueTask`) end-to-end with `CancellationToken` on I/O. `async void` only for true event
  handlers; every fire-and-forget (`_ = ...`) must observe errors (log/`ErrorReporter`). Library/infrastructure code
  that does not need the UI sync context uses `ConfigureAwait(false)`.
- Exceptions are not control flow. Do not catch-and-swallow; expected API cases use `AppException` / `ApiResult`;
  unexpected failures go through `ISystemErrorLogger` / `ErrorReporter`.
- Startup/DI: register cheaply; defer DB/network/file/heavy init until first use (migrations/seed stay on API startup as
  today). Do not load every page VM or catalog at process start.

## JSON
- Every API request/response type (and each closed `PagedResult<T>` / `CursorPage<T>` / list) is listed in
  `Contracts/Common/ContractsJsonContext` (compile-time metadata on both sides). A missing type still works through the
  reflection fallback, but its first call is slower.

## EF Core and API reads
- Reads that do not update entities: `AsNoTracking()`. Page with `PageQueryExecutor` / `KeysetPaging` (or the feature’s
  existing pager); never return unbounded lists to the UI.
- Numbered list totals stop at `PageQuery.MaxCountedTotal` (pass the unsorted filtered query to `ToPageAsync`);
  the desktop shows "10,000+" via `DisplayTotal`. Everyday query shapes are run once at startup (`DatabaseWarmUp`).
- No N+1: shape list queries with a single projection/`Select`, or an intentional `Include`/`AsSplitQuery` when needed.
  Do not load graphs larger than the screen needs.
- Application stays free of EF types; query details live in Infrastructure repositories.
